# Portfolio conventions

This is Zobeda Dewan's personal portfolio: React 19 + TypeScript + Tailwind v4
+ Vite, deployed on Vercel. It is a hiring artifact — a recruiter reading it is
also judging the code, so the site must practise what it claims.

## Content rules — these are not style preferences

1. **Never invent statistics, metrics, testimonials, client names or logos.**
   Every number on this site traces to the resume. If a design calls for social
   proof that does not exist ("50+ projects", "99% satisfaction", a quote from a
   named person), leave it out and say so. Do not fill it with plausible copy.
2. **All user-facing copy lives in `src/data/`.** Never hardcode prose in a
   component. Sections read from `profile.ts`, `experience.ts`, `skills.ts`,
   `process.ts`, `projects.ts`.
3. **No contact form.** A form needs a backend and fails silently. Use `mailto:`.
4. **No phone number on the page.** It stays in the PDF resume only.

## Design system

Tokens are defined in `src/index.css` under `@theme`. Use the Tailwind classes
they generate (`text-ink`, `bg-blush`, `border-petal`) — never a raw hex value
and never a default Tailwind palette colour (`purple-600`, `pink-500`, …).

| Token | Role |
|---|---|
| `cream` | page background |
| `blush` | alternating section bands, card fills |
| `petal` | borders, dividers, chip fills |
| `ink` / `ink-soft` | headings / body copy |
| `rose` / `rose-deep` | action colour / hover and tinted-ground text |
| `plum` | depth, timeline spine, secondary |
| `gold` / `gold-deep` | decorative accents / small accent text |

**Contrast rules, already measured — do not break them:**

- Text on `blush` or `petal` uses `rose-deep`, never `rose` (rose is 4.35:1 on
  blush, which fails AA for body text).
- `gold` never carries small text — it is 3.8:1 on cream. Eyebrow labels and
  any text under 18px use `gold-deep`. `gold` is for rules, borders and large
  numerals.
- White on the `gradient-brand` (rose→plum) is fine: 4.95:1 at its weakest.
- **Never apply an opacity modifier to a text colour** (`text-ink-soft/80`,
  `text-gold opacity-60`). The ratios above assume full strength, and fading
  a token silently drops it below AA. Both contrast bugs found in this
  codebase were exactly this. Fade backgrounds and borders, never text.

**Typography:** `font-display` (Fraunces) for h1–h3 and the wordmark;
`font-sans` (Plus Jakarta Sans) for body; `font-mono` (JetBrains Mono) for
eyebrow labels, numerals and tech chips. The mono is the site's "IT-focused"
signal — keep using it. No script or handwritten fonts.

**Custom utilities:** `gradient-brand`, `text-gradient`, `eyebrow`. The brand
gradient appears only on the wordmark, primary buttons, section numerals and
timeline nodes — keeping it scarce is what makes it read as a signature.

## Layout restraint

- **One decorative element per section, maximum.** A corner bloom *or* a rule
  *or* a floating chip — not all three.
- **Prefer a two-column split** over any layout that spreads content across
  three zones.
- When asked to "simplify", change the *arrangement* — do not delete copy.
  If cutting content seems warranted, ask first.

## Accessibility — non-negotiable, it is a claim the site makes

- One `<h1>` per page; headings in order.
- Every image has real `alt`; every icon-only link has `aria-label`; decorative
  SVGs get `aria-hidden="true"`.
- All motion is gated on `prefers-reduced-motion` via
  `usePrefersReducedMotion`. Scroll reveals go through `<Reveal>`, which has a
  2s failsafe so content can never stay invisible.
- Focus is visible everywhere (global `:focus-visible` in `index.css`).
- The mobile menu traps Tab, closes on Escape, and restores focus.

## Adding a project

Append one entry to the `projects` array in `src/data/projects.ts` and drop
images in `public/projects/<slug>/`. The home-page grid, the `/work/<slug>`
route and `sitemap.xml` all derive from it. Nothing else needs editing.
